@flexiarg creativity-commons/institutions-are-products
@title Institutions Are Products
@keywords recursion, institutional creativity, rules as tests, institutions for building institutions
@audience institution designers, agent operators, communities of practice
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Corneli 2016 (ICCC 2016): the recursive reading that runs through principles 3 ("changing the rules means writing new tests"), 7 (Producers' rights to devise institutions), 8 ("many of the features that make up a creative ecosystem must themselves be produced"), the Ostrom 2009 p. 19 quotation under 2B, the two-part ICCC example, and the conclusion. Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29. Derived from the paper, not mined from runs.
@status draft 2026-10-29; runs correct it
@references [creativity-commons/ARGUMENT creativity-commons/affected-participants-change-the-rules creativity-commons/recognised-right-to-self-organise creativity-commons/nested-places institutions/rules-as-institutional-statements]

! conclusion:
  In a creativity commons the institutions are themselves Products: the
  rules, tests and arenas that govern contribution are made by Producers
  in the commons, and the creativity design principles apply to making
  them as much as to making anything else.

  + context:
    Ostrom's principles describe institutions that users devised; the
    2016 transposition makes that devising part of creative work. Rules
    are if-then statements, so each contains a test, and writing a new
    rule is writing a new test. The paper's ICCC example reads an
    existing institution (a conference) through the principles and then
    proposes new ones (post-publication review, shared open problems,
    challenge problems with evaluation metrics).

  + IF:
    A community, human, agent or mixed, does repeated work under rules
    that it could change.

  + HOWEVER:
    An institution that its members can rewrite at will governs nothing;
    one they cannot rewrite stops fitting the work. And early on, the
    programmer or operator is the main external authority over agents.

  + THEN:
    Treat each institutional feature as a Product with its own
    Producers, Process and Place: record who may change a rule, write
    the rule as a test, let those affected propose changes with the case
    that motivated them, have a non-beneficiary review the change, and
    count how often changes come from the system itself rather than from
    outside.

  + BECAUSE:
    "Our institutions – including institutions for building
    institutions – are analysable in programmatic terms" (Corneli 2016,
    conclusion). If they are analysable as programs, they can be
    produced, tested and revised like programs, by the same commons
    that works under them. In the paper's quotation of Ostrom: "How
    about if you do A in the future, and I will do B, and before we ever
    make a decision about C again, we both discuss it and make a joint
    decision?"

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Example: the FUTON Emacs fix rule and checker were an institution written by claude-16; fix agents found six checker defects and each was fixed in the shared check. Producers changed the test they worked under.]
    next[Two directions (Joe, 2026-10-29): with people, draw the institution out of how maintainers, students or professors already work; with agents, have them draft new dispatch institutions when the current ones handle a case badly.]
    next[Compare with Alinery playbooks (futon3/library/efficiency/REVIEW-alinery-2026-10-19.md): can participants revise the playbook they run under?]
